WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.350
Hi, I'm Erik Voss, and we've been hearing
a lot about this Han Solo Star Wars

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:08.309
movie, how director is Phil Lord and
Chris Miller were fired and replaced

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:11.910
with Ron Howard. Now, replacing a director
in the middle of production has

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:16.289
become more of a trend in recent years
with Disney properties, with Han Solo, but

00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:20.460
also, rumor has it with Rogue One. Gareth
Edwards reportedly stepped back during

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:24.689
reshoots to allow Tony Gilroy to direct
large parts of the movie. And this has

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:28.260
also happened with Marvel movies, which
are also owned by Disney. With Ant-Man,

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.489
initial writer-director, Edgar Wright,
left the project after Marvel head, Kevin

00:00:32.489 --> 00:00:36.930
Feige, had a new, less edgy, script
written without Wright's input. Similarly,

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:41.340
Patty Jenkins, before she directed Wonder
Woman for DC, was initially hired to

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:45.629
direct Thor: The Dark World for Disney/Marvel, but then she was fired, too. And

00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:50.070
this brings us to the real, deep-rooted
reason why Disney-owned studios, like

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:54.090
Marvel and Lucasfilm, keep clashing with
these directors. I think that when it

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:58.500
comes to the way the studio views its
big film franchises, Disney hates

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.510
originality. Now, what do I mean by that?
Or, as you may be thinking, how dare you?

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:08.820
Sure, Disney is the brand of dreams and
imagination, and over the decades it

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.380
has often led the way in creative
risk-taking, both in its storytelling and

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:17.580
the way it runs its business, but let's
be honest: Disney is not the studio where

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.229
filmmakers go to be original, to buck the
trend, to express their unique voice as

00:01:22.229 --> 00:01:26.280
storytellers. Like, it's no secret that
the classic Disney films that we know

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:30.060
and love were really just
family-friendly adaptations of darker

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:34.829
Grimm's Brothers tales. And even though
Pixar, which Disney also owns, has been

00:01:34.829 --> 00:01:39.360
known for more innovative, original
stories, that's only because Pixar fought

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.470
very hard for that creative independence
from Disney. They actually have a list of

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:48.299
storytelling rules that intentionally
defy the Disney model. Now yes, people

00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:52.530
like Kathleen Kennedy at Lucasfilm and
Kevin Feige at Marvel do have

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:56.969
independence in the way they run their
studios, but a lot of their success comes

00:01:56.969 --> 00:02:01.320
from the fact that they share Disney's
creative mindset and general risk

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.189
aversion. Like, as much as I have loved
Marvel under Feige's direction, a common

00:02:05.189 --> 00:02:09.119
complaint is that each movie feels like
the same kind of fun adventure, where

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:13.200
nothing truly dark ever happens. And Edgar Wright trying to go against that with Ant-man...

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.250
an insider put it this way - Kevin Feige
and his top lieutenants run Marvel with

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:21.540
a singularity of vision, but when you
take a true auteur and throw him into

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.900
the mix, this is what you get. They don't
want you to speak up too much or have

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.930
too much vision.
Similarly, the new Disney/Star Wars

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:32.310
movies have been hugely successful, but
some would argue only by sticking close

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:36.000
to the script laid out by the original
trilogy. Like, JJ Abrams was given way

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.599
more control over The Force Awakens, but
it was probably really easy for Kennedy

00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:43.200
to do that because the movie Abrams wanted to make followed the tone and the

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.459
story beats of A New Hope pretty closely.
Meanwhile, whenever these studios try to

00:02:47.459 --> 00:02:51.690
partner with directors who are known for
their originality and boldness, their

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:55.739
tendency to resist studio input, even if
it risks alienating parts of the

00:02:55.739 --> 00:03:00.120
audience, these days Disney is far more
inclined just to sever ties. Let's look

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:04.049
at Lord and Miller with the Han Solo movie.
So, Lord and Miller are comedy guys. They

00:03:04.049 --> 00:03:07.709
directed the Lego Movie and the 21 Jump
Street movies, and a lot of the jokes in

00:03:07.709 --> 00:03:11.760
those films come from Lord and Miller
encouraging the actors to improvise and

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.250
go off script.
So improv can be a really effective way

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:18.690
to make the comedy in a movie feel
organic and truly as funny as possible.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:22.500
Directors like Judd Apatow and Adam
McKay use improvisation a lot in their

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.609
films. But it sounds like that process did
not gel with the expectations of

00:03:26.609 --> 00:03:30.269
Kathleen Kennedy and Lawrence Kasdan, who
wrote the script. Of course, everyone just

00:03:30.269 --> 00:03:35.180
chalked it up to creative differences,
which is the explanation everyone uses, but...

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:40.410
here's what I heard.
So sources inside the production claimed

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:45.810
that the crew was annoyed by the slow
process of doing multiple improvised

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:49.980
takes and that the humor was getting a
little too meta. Some people also said

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:54.450
that the acting from Han Solo actor,
Alden Ehrenreich, suffered and that they

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:57.690
had to bring in an acting coach because
his performance was becoming too much

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:01.680
like Ace Ventura. What does that even mean?
Is there a scene were Han was like, fighting

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:07.230
the Sarlacc, and then walks out of the
room and turns to Lando, like, do not go

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:13.980
in there, Woo! I guess it's not very good.
Now another source said that Kathleen

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.190
Kennedy just wasn't a fan of the way
that Lord and Miller were doing things

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:21.900
from the beginning, hating the way they
fold socks. Which is a bummer, because have

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:26.280
you seen the way Lord and Miller fold
socks? So fresh and

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.760
original! Now, I don't know how many of
these rumors are actually true. For all

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.810
we know, they could just be leaks by
insiders at Disney or Lucasfilm to

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:37.889
justify this abrupt firing of directors
in the middle of shooting, which almost

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:41.880
never happens with Hollywood movies. But
here's what I do know: Lawrence Kasdan is

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.930
a screenwriting legend who co-wrote The
Empire Strikes Back, Return of the Jedi

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:50.460
and The Force Awakens, so yeah, he's kind
of the authority on the character of Han

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:54.750
Solo, and I honestly wouldn't blame Kasdan if he was frustrated that Lord and

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:59.640
Miller were telling Ehrenreich to ad lib
new Han Solo dialogue to replace the lines

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:03.900
that he wrote in the script.
Han Solo isn't a new, undefined character

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:09.300
that filmmakers can organically develop
on the fly. He's a film icon with a very

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:13.380
specific voice that Kasdan and Disney
are understandably very protective of.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:17.460
But the frustrating thing about this is
this isn't Lord and Miller's fault.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:21.240
Lucasfilm and Disney knew exactly what
they were getting when they hired those

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:25.260
two, just like Marvel did when they
initially hired Edgar Wright, the bold

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:29.729
innovator who made Shaun of the Dead, Hot
Fuzz and Scott Pilgrim vs. the World to make

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:33.750
Ant-man. Actually, back when that firing
happened, Joss Whedon said that he read

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:37.050
Edgar Wright's script and said it was
the best script that he had read for the

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:39.960
Marvel Universe to that point. And I've
always felt bummed that we never got to

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:42.870
see that movie. And now I'm kind of
feeling the same way about Lord and

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:47.760
Miller's Han Solo movie. Look, even if
their movie felt tonally off and too

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.050
improv-y and meta, with like, Han talking
out of his butt as he solves pet-related

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:56.640
crimes, I still really want to see that
Han Solo! And come on, if Disney is worried

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:01.380
that a weird take on Han Solo would ruin
the Star Wars franchise, obviously that

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:05.010
wouldn't at all be the worst thing to
happen to Star Wars. Unfortunately, we'll

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:08.610
never know what Lord and Miller's movie
will look like because Disney and

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:12.630
Lucasfilm have replaced them with Ron
Howard. Now, Ron Howard is a fine director.

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:16.979
He made Apollo 13, and he won the Oscar
for A Beautiful Mind, he also directed

00:06:16.979 --> 00:06:21.270
The Da Vinci Code movies. Yeah, you know,
when Hanks rocked a mullet. Also, it

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:24.840
seems like Star Wars has been near and
dear to Ron Howard's heart for a long

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:29.700
time. In 1999, he wrote a really pissed
off letter to critics, defending Jacob

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:33.450
Lloyd's acting in the Phantom Menace. Of
course, that letter also called Phantom

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:36.480
Menace truly amazing. Umm...

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:40.680
I guess from this point on, if you keep
throwing shade at Alden Ehrenreich's

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:44.400
acting in Han Solo, expect an angry letter
from Ron Howard. I don't really know how

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:48.420
much of a vision Ron Howard has for this
Han Solo movie, or how much of a vision

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.000
he really could conceive this late in
the production process.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:55.110
It sounds like Disney and Lucasfilm
hired him because he's a safe option. Ron

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:59.760
Howard is a veteran director who will
probably honor the vision of Han Solo as

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:03.330
he's laid out in Kasdan's script, and if
you just want a smooth production

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:07.140
process so this Han Solo movie comes
together in one piece, I guess that's

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:10.950
good news. Like, this means we'll get a
more purely Lawrence Kasdan Han Solo

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:15.990
movie now, and that could be perfect. On
the other hand, I feel like Disney's war

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:20.310
against originality has stifled a
potentially new and bold take on Han

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:24.150
Solo. One that could be way better than
the one we end up getting. Remember when

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:28.830
George Lucas made the first Star Wars,
that movie was nothing like any of these

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:32.610
sci-fi fantasy movies that came out in
the seventies. It was a huge risk for the

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:36.270
studio at the time. There were all kinds
of production problems - shooting delays,

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:39.720
going way over budget.
All for this weird, quirky movie about a

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:44.430
space religion and walking carpet. My
point is the original Star Wars worked

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:48.840
because it was original. It became the
biggest movie franchise of all time, and

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:53.400
the concern now is film studios like
Disney have become so successful at

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:57.510
putting out consistent, mass appeal
franchises, all by sticking to a proven

00:07:57.510 --> 00:08:02.430
formula, that they now can't stand the
idea of a bold new director allowing his

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:06.990
or her voice to change and evolve those
franchises. And ultimately that could

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:10.320
take some of the joy and excitement out
of the movies, leaving us with our socks

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.490
folded by Ron Howard. Do you think it was
fair that Phil Lord and Chris Miller got

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:18.300
fired? Or to look at things a different
way, were Lord and Miller justified in

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:22.380
diverging from Kasdan's script to go
with a more improvisational version of

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:25.200
Han Solo? Like, do you think they could
have damaged the character's reputation?

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:28.830
And do you think Disney plays things too
safe in general? Like, I don't need to

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:32.640
come off anti-Disney at all, but I do
think they would benefit from letting

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:36.000
their directors be more truly original.
Anyway, that's just my take ,but I'm

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.970
curious to know what you think. Let me
know down in the comments and hit me up

